course: biology
lesson: 265
title: Tyrannosaurus rex: what we really know, from teeth and dung to tiny arms
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tyrannosaurus rex lived about 68 to 66 million years ago in western North America, and a grown one was about 12 metres long and as tall at the hips as a double-decker bus.
explain: Nobody ever saw a living T. rex, so everything we know comes from clues: bones, teeth, footprints, skin prints and even fossil dung full of crushed bone.
explain: Its teeth were as long as a banana and thick like a railway spike, made to crush bone, and its bite was one of the strongest of any land animal that ever lived.
explain: Films like to show a T. rex racing a jeep and missing you if you stand still, but its heavy legs could not sprint, and its big forward-facing eyes and keen nose would have found you easily.
explain: Bones even have growth lines like the rings in a tree, and they tell us a T. rex grew up fast as a teenager and lived to about 28 to 30 years.
fact: T. rex lived about 68 to 66 million years ago in western North America and was one of the last big dinosaurs.
fact: A grown T. rex was about 12 metres long; its biggest teeth, root included, were about 30 centimetres long and kept being replaced all its life.
fact: T. rex teeth were made to crush bone, and its bite was many times stronger than a lion's.
fact: Fossil dung is called a coprolite; one T. rex coprolite, as big as a loaf of bread, was full of crushed bone from a plant-eater.
fact: T. rex arms were short, about as long as a man's arm, but strong, with two fingers; scientists still argue what they were for.
fact: A grown T. rex probably could not truly run fast, perhaps 15 to 25 kilometres an hour, because its legs would have broken under its weight; young ones were slimmer and faster.
fact: The idea that T. rex cannot see you if you stand still comes from a film; its big forward-facing eyes judged distance well and its sense of smell was very good.
fact: Nobody knows what T. rex sounded like, because sounds do not fossilise; its relatives, birds and crocodiles, boom and rumble with closed mouths, so it may have rumbled rather than roared.
fact: Sue, found in South Dakota in 1990, is one of the most complete T. rex skeletons, with about 90 percent of the bones, a treasure because most dinosaurs are known only from a few bones; nobody knows whether Sue was male or female.
fact: Skin prints show small scales on parts of T. rex, while some smaller relatives such as Yutyrannus had feathers; babies may have been fluffy, but this is not known for sure.
fact: Stegosaurus lived about 150 million years ago, so more time separates Stegosaurus from T. rex than separates T. rex from us.
fact: Growth lines in T. rex bones show it lived to about 28 to 30 years, and as a teenager it gained up to two kilograms a day.
exercise: When did Tyrannosaurus rex live? | About 68 to 66 million years ago | About 150 million years ago | About 10,000 years ago | About 300 million years ago
exercise: Where did T. rex live? | In western North America | In Australia | In Antarctica only | In southern Africa
exercise: About how long was a grown T. rex? | About 12 metres | About 2 metres | About 50 metres | About 100 metres
exercise: About how long were the biggest T. rex teeth, root included? | About 30 centimetres, like a banana | About 1 centimetre long, the size of a pea | About 2 metres, like a door | About 5 centimetres, like a finger
exercise: What were T. rex teeth best made for? | Crushing bone | Chewing leaves | Sucking up water | Cracking nuts
exercise: What is a coprolite? | Fossil dung | A fossil egg | A fossil tooth | A fossil footprint
exercise: What did scientists find inside a T. rex coprolite? | Crushed bone from a plant-eater | Seeds and leaves | Fish scales only | Small stones it swallowed for fun
exercise: What were T. rex arms like? | Short but strong, with two fingers | Short and weak, with no fingers | Long and thin, with five fingers | As long as its legs, with three claws
exercise: How fast could a grown T. rex probably move? | It walked quickly but could not truly run fast | It could easily outrun a car speeding along on a motorway | It could not move at all, only stand | It ran fastest of all dinosaurs
exercise: What would happen if you stood very still in front of a T. rex? | It would still see and smell you | It could not see you at all | It would fall asleep | It would think you were a tree and walk off
exercise: How do we know what T. rex sounded like? | We do not know, because sounds do not fossilise | From the old recordings of it found sealed in rock | From its roar shown in films | From a T. rex that lived in a zoo
exercise: Who is Sue? | One of the most complete T. rex skeletons ever found | The famous scientist who first gave T. rex its name | A Stegosaurus standing in a museum | The last living T. rex
exercise: What do T. rex skin prints show? | Small scales on parts of its body | Long bright feathers all over its body | Smooth skin like a frog | Fur like a bear
exercise: Did T. rex ever meet Stegosaurus? | No, Stegosaurus lived about 80 million years earlier | Yes, they fought each other all the time over food and land | Yes, they lived in the same herd | No, Stegosaurus lived after T. rex
exercise: How do scientists know how old a T. rex was when it died? | By counting growth lines in its bones, like tree rings | By counting its teeth | By measuring its tail | By the colour of its skin
exercise: How much weight could a teenage T. rex put on in a day? | Up to two kilograms | Up to two hundred kilograms | Only a few grams | Nothing, it stopped growing at ten
test: «my cousin says T. rex had massive teeth for eating leaves, that's why they're so big» What is right? | No, its thick teeth were for crushing bone and meat | Yes, big thick teeth are the best ones for chewing leaves | Yes, T. rex only ate leaves and fruit | No, T. rex had no teeth, only a beak
test: In a film a T. rex chases a jeep at top speed and nearly catches it. What is closer to the truth? | A grown T. rex was too heavy to run that fast | A T. rex could run much faster than any car | A T. rex could fly right over the top of the jeep | A T. rex could only crawl on its belly
test: «stand still mate, it can't see you if you don't move!!» Is that right? | No, T. rex had good eyes and a keen nose | Yes, it only saw moving things | Yes, but only at night | No, because T. rex was totally blind
test: A cartoon dinosaur film shows T. rex and Stegosaurus fighting. What is wrong with that? | They lived about 80 million years apart and never met | Stegosaurus was much bigger and stronger than T. rex | They were the same animal with two names | T. rex was far too small to fight a Stegosaurus at all
test: Count them: a museum hall has 3 T. rex skeletons. How many legs, arms and tails is that in all? | 6 legs, 6 arms and 3 tails | 12 legs, 3 arms and 3 tails | 6 legs, 3 arms and 6 tails | 3 legs, 6 arms and 3 tails
test: «lol T. rex with them tiny useless arms, couldn't even scratch its nose» What is true? | Its arms were short but strong, and scientists still argue what they were for | Its arms were useless, very weak and could not even lift a feather | Its arms fell off as it grew up | Its arms were longer than its legs
test: Why is fossil dung so useful to scientists? | It shows what the animal really ate | It shows how fast the animal ran | It shows what colour the animal was | It shows how loud the animal roared
test: A T. rex in a cartoon opens its mouth and roars like a lion. What do scientists think? | Nobody knows its voice; it may have rumbled with its mouth shut like birds and crocodiles | It roared exactly like a lion, and scientists have recordings of the sound | It squeaked like a mouse | It sang like a songbird
test: Why is a nearly whole skeleton like Sue such a treasure? | Most dinosaurs are known only from a few bones | It still had all its skin and its eyes | It was found alive | It is the only T. rex that has ever been found anywhere
test: «so did T. rex have feathers or not, just tell me» What is the honest answer? | Skin prints show scales on parts of grown ones, though babies may have been fluffy | It was covered all over in long colourful feathers, like a giant parrot | It had thick fur like a woolly mammoth | It had smooth skin with no scales at all
test: What would happen if a grown T. rex walked into your school yard? | Its hips would be as high as a double-decker bus | It would be no taller than a pony | It would be much taller than a skyscraper in a big city | It would fit under a table
test: A T. rex was about 29 when it died. What did its bones show? | Growth lines that can be counted like tree rings | Its exact age written in numbers on its teeth | Nothing at all, because age cannot be known from bones | That it was still a baby
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which the teeth of Tyrannosaurus rex were adapted. | The crushing of bone and the tearing of flesh | The grinding of vegetation | The filtering of water | The cracking of seeds and nuts
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the speed of an adult Tyrannosaurus rex. | It is understood to have been incapable of true rapid running on account of its great mass | It is understood to have exceeded the speed of a motor vehicle travelling upon a modern road | It is understood to have been entirely immobile | It is understood to have been the swiftest of all dinosaurs
exam: Ascertain the nature of a coprolite, as the term is employed in palaeontology. | Fossilised dung | A fossilised egg | A fossilised tooth | A fossilised footprint
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the chronological relationship between Stegosaurus and Tyrannosaurus rex? | They were separated by a greater span of time than that separating Tyrannosaurus rex from the present day | They were contemporaries inhabiting the same region | Stegosaurus succeeded Tyrannosaurus rex by some millions of years | They were separated by no more than a few centuries
